    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `extract_invoice` in `app/invoices.py`. `pytest -q` passes (2 tests), but neither test calls `extract_invoice`, and I didn't send a real request to the API. So the code is untested against a live response.\n\n**What it does:**\n- Sends one `client.beta.messages.create` request to `MODEL` with `max_tokens=1024`. The invoice goes in as a document block via `invoice_block(file_id)`, the same way `summarize_invoice` does it, followed by a short instruction to extract the fields.\n- Uses structured outputs: `output_config={\"format\": {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\": INVOICE_SCHEMA}}`. The API itself constrains the reply to the schema, and the prompt doesn't ask for JSON.\n- Joins the reply's text blocks and returns them decoded with `json.loads`. Only text blocks are kept because `claude-opus-5-5` always thinks, so the reply can also contain thinking blocks.\n\n**Two things I added that you didn't ask for:**\n- **A second beta flag.** The request sends `STRUCTURED_OUTPUTS_BETA = \"structured-outputs-2025-12-15\"` alongside `FILES_BETA`. The installed SDK's own structured-output helper on the beta endpoint adds this flag automatically, so I matched it.\n- **An error when the reply stops early.** If `stop_reason` is anything but `end_turn` (for example a refusal or hitting the token limit), the function raises a `RuntimeError` instead of returning. The schema guarantee doesn't hold for those replies, and the ledger import rejects rows with missing keys, so failing loudly seemed safer than returning a partial row."
  },
